          %h2#definition Definition
          %p
            A
            %strong lattice
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/partial+order"} poset
            which admits all finite
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/meets"} meets
            and finite
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/joins"} joins
            (or all finite
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/products"} products
            and finite
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coproducts"} coproducts
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            (a
            = succeed "))." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%280%2C1%29-category"} (0,1)-category
          %p
            As
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%280%2C1%29-limits"} (0,1)-limits
            are meets and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%280%2C1%29-colimits"} (0,1)-colimits
            are joins, a lattice is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%280%2C1%29-category"} (0,1)-category
            that is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finitely+complete+category"} finitely complete
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finitely+cocomplete+category"} finitely cocomplete
          %p
            A
            %strong lattice
            can also be defined as an algebraic structure, with the binary operations
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∧
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \wedge
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∨
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \vee
            and the constants
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⊤
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \top
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ⊥
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bot
            = succeed "\u2019," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/top"} top
            = succeed "\u2019.)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bottom"} bottom
          %ul
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ∧
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \wedge
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ∨
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \vee
              are each
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/idempotent"} idempotent
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+magma"} commutative
              = succeed ";" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/associative+magma"} associative
            %li
              the
              = succeed ":" do
                %em absorption laws
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi a
                      %mo ∨
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi a
                      %mo ∧
                      %mi b
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mi a
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \vee (a \wedge b) = a
              = succeed ";" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi a
                      %mo ∧
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi a
                      %mo ∨
                      %mi b
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mi a
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \wedge (a \vee b) = a
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ⊤
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \top
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ⊥
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bot
              are the respective
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identity+element"} identities
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ∧
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \wedge
              and
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ∨
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \vee
          %p
            You can recover the original poset from either the meet or the join;
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo ≤
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \leq b
            iff
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo ∧
                    %mi b
                    %mo =
                    %mi a
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \wedge b = a
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi b
                  %mo ≤
                  %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b \leq a
            iff
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo ∨
                    %mi b
                    %mo =
                    %mi a
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \vee b = a
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo ∧
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a\wedge b
            is the greatest lower bound for
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo ∨
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \vee b
            is the least upper bound for
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi b
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ≤
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \leq
            agree.) Indeed, we may say that a lattice is a
            %em bisemilattice
            in that it has two semilattice structures that are compatible in that they define (but in dual ways) the same partial order.
          %p
            Note that a poset with only finite meets
            %em or
            finite joins is a (meet- or join-)
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semilattice"} semilattice
            %em all
            joins and meets (not just finitary ones) is a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complete+lattice"} complete lattice
          %h2#bounded_lattices_and_pseudolattices Bounded lattices and pseudolattices
          %p
            Traditionally, a lattice need have only finite
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inhabited+set"} inhabited
            meets and joins; that is, it need not have a top or bottom element. Algebraically, this means
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∧
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \wedge
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∨
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \vee
            need not have identities.
          %p
            Then one may call a lattice that
            %em does
            have a top and a bottom a
            = succeed ";" do
              %strong bounded lattice
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bounded+poset"} bounded poset
            is a poset that has top and bottom elements.
          %p
            The other approach is to define a lattice, as above, to require a top and a bottom and then use the term
            %strong pseudolattice
            to allow for the possibility that it might not.
          %p
            From an algebraic point of view, requiring top and bottom is quite natural, a special case of preferring
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoids"} monoids
            to more general
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semigroups"} semigroups
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} -\infty
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+line"} real line
            makes it no longer a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field"} field
            (addition is especially problematic); more generally, a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Banach+lattice"} Banach lattice
            need not (and, except in one degenerate case, cannot) have a top or a bottom. In fact, adjoining top and bottom to any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/total+order"} totally
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordered+ring"} ordered ring
            makes it no longer a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ring"} ring
          %h2#lattice_homomorphisms Lattice homomorphisms
          %p
            A lattice homomorphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            from a lattice
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            to a lattice
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function"} function
            from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            (seen as sets) that preserves
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∧
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \wedge
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∨
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \vee
            (and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⊤
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \top
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ⊥
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bot
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ∧
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∧
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo ⊤
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mo ⊤
                  %mo ,
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ∨
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∨
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo ⊥
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mo ⊥
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(x \wedge y) = f(x) \wedge f(y),\; f(\top) = \top,\; f(x \vee y) = f(x) \vee f(y),\; f(\bot) = \bot .
          %p
            Note that such a homomorphism is necessarily a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monotone+function"} monotone function
          %p
            Thus, a lattice is a poset (or even a semilattice) with
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/property-like+structure"} property-like structure
          %p
            Lattices and lattice homomorphims form a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/concrete+category"} concrete category
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lat"} Lat
